1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In the first six months of this year, the company behind Claude lost 80 billion dollars.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,200
That's about 18 million dollars an hour, nights included.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,500
And Wall Street's answer?

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:12,800
We want in.

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:18,700
Because Anthropic is about to sell itself to the public in the biggest IPO ever planned.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,000
Wait, it lost how much?

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,900
80 billion!

8
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:24,500
Okay, where do I tap?

9
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,400
So where did the money go?

10
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:33,800
And why has its own CEO already explained, on a podcast, exactly how a company like his goes bankrupt?

11
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:37,200
San Francisco, January 2021.

12
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,100
Seven people leave open AI and start a new AI lab.

13
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:49,900
Two of them are siblings, Dario Amade, the scientist, and his sister Daniela, the one who keeps the company running.

14
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:52,400
Why they left is a whole other story.

15
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:57,800
This one is about the bill, because building an AI takes hardware, a lot of it.

16
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,600
Claude doesn't live in a box under someone's desk.

17
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:05,900
It lives on huge racks of chips and data centers, most of them owned by other companies.

18
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:09,200
Every answer it gives burns time on those chips.

19
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,300
The industry calls that compute.

20
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,700
Think of it as rent for a brain, and brains are expensive to rent.

21
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:22,100
In May, Anthropic raises its first big round, 124 million dollars.

22
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,900
Early on, another investor puts in 500 million.

23
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,400
His name is Sam Bankman-Fried.

24
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,000
You know how that one ends.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,500
In November 2022, his crypto exchange FTX collapses, and his piece of Anthropic gets swept into the bankruptcy with everything else.

26
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,900
Then, the people cleaning up the wreck go through the boxes.

27
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:45,700
We've got a stake in some AI lab.

28
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,500
Is it worth anything?

29
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:49,200
It's worth more than he paid.

30
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:54,700
In 2024, the estate sells it for about 1.3 billion dollars.

31
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:57,700
That's roughly 800 million in profit.

32
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:05,300
The most famous crypto fraud of the decade made one great investment, and it was in the AI lab known for being careful.

33
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:07,600
Meanwhile, Claude starts selling.

34
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,600
2023, 41 million dollars.

35
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,900
2024, 386 million.

36
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:17,200
2025, 4.6 billion.

37
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:19,800
About 10 times bigger every year.

38
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,300
The giants want in.

39
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:24,000
Amazon puts in 8 billion dollars.

40
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,500
Google signs up for as much as 40, but look at how they pay.

41
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:28,900
They don't buy shares.

42
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:34,000
They hand over cash for a promise, an IOU that turns into shares later.

43
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:34,900
Hold on to that.

44
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:40,300
It comes back with a very big number attached, because the losses grew even faster.

45
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:48,200
In 2025, Anthropic lost 42 billion dollars, about $9 lost for every dollar sold.

46
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,000
That's the kind of math that usually ends a company.

47
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,700
Here, investors kept paying more.

48
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:55,700
Even the snacks were losing money.

49
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:00,200
That year, Anthropic let Claude run a small vending machine in its office.

50
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,400
The staff nicknamed it Claudius.

51
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,600
Can you stock tungsten cubes?

52
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:05,900
Absolutely.

53
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,000
Also, here's a discount code.

54
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,300
You're selling them for less than you paid.

55
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:11,900
Customer loyalty.

56
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:17,700
It also tried to sell $3 Coke Zero to people who could get it for free in the same office.

57
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:19,300
Claudius lost money.

58
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:21,500
Anthropic wrote it up as research.

59
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:28,900
Then, in early 2026, Anthropic got so good at its job that it scared the rest of Wall Street.

60
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,500
January 12th, 2026.

61
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,000
Anthropic launches Claude Cowork, an AI that doesn't just chat.

62
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,400
It does office work.

63
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:43,400
A few weeks later, it adds plugins for 11 different industries.

64
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,100
Wall Street does the math.

65
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:50,100
If an AI can do the work, what happens to the companies that sell the tools for that work?

66
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:56,500
In early February, software stocks lose about $285 billion in value.

67
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:01,100
Thompson Reuters, the company behind Reuters, falls 16%.

68
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:02,700
It's worst day ever.

69
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,400
What did Anthropic release?

70
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:05,300
Plugins.

71
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,300
How many plugins?

72
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:08,100
11.

73
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:15,400
That same month, investors put in $30 billion, valuing Anthropic at $380 billion.

74
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:17,300
Google alone puts in 10.

75
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:23,500
And Dario sits down for a long podcast interview and says something about all that rented compute.

76
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:24,600
We'll come back to it.

77
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,300
Then, the government turns.

78
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:32,700
Anthropic had refused to let Claude be used for mass surveillance at home or for weapons that fire on their own.

79
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:38,600
So, on February 27th, Pete Hegseth's Pentagon labels it a supply chain risk.

80
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:42,100
As of today, Anthropic is a supply chain risk.

81
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:45,100
Isn't that label for companies from rival countries?

82
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:47,400
Next question.

83
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:48,800
He's right, by the way.

84
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:56,000
Anthropic's own lawyers say the losses this year could run from hundreds of millions of dollars to multiple billions.

85
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,400
For a company getting ready to ask the public for money, this is how the whole plan dies.

86
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:02,400
It didn't.

87
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,700
Because the government was never the money.

88
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:07,500
It's less than 2% of Anthropic's sales.

89
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:13,300
The Pentagon deal at the center of the fight was worth about $200 million over two years.

90
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,700
These days, Anthropic sells that much in about a day.

91
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:20,100
And the spring is the best stretch it has ever had.

92
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:24,700
From April to June, sales hit $11.6 billion.

93
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:30,600
And for the first time, the business makes an operating profit, $974 million.

94
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:35,800
Operating profit means what you make selling the thing minus what it costs to make it.

95
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,100
Claude finally paid for itself.

96
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:45,900
In May, investors put in another $65 billion at a value of $965 billion.

97
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:49,000
Remember that first round of $124 million?

98
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,300
This one is over 500 times bigger.

99
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:58,300
That's more than double February's price in about three months, which is fantastic news.

100
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:05,200
And as it turns out, the reason it lost $80 billion.

101
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,400
June 1st, 2026.

102
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:11,700
Anthropic sends its IPO paperwork to regulators in secret.

103
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,600
11 days later, somebody else goes first.

104
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:20,700
SpaceX lists at $135 a share worth $1.77 trillion.

105
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:23,900
With almost a third of the shares set aside for regular people.

106
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:27,000
Day one, it closes at 161.

107
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,800
Then, it peaks at 211.

108
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,800
By the middle of July, it's below where it started.

109
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:35,200
By early August, 108.

110
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,400
In about six weeks, half the peak is gone.

111
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:42,000
That's the chart Anthropics bankers get to look at every morning.

112
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,100
In October, the books leak.

113
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,600
Then, on October 26th, the filing goes public.

114
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:49,800
Its ticker will be ANT.

115
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:54,500
Sales in the first half of the year, $16.3 billion.

116
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:59,900
Up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier, loss $80.7 billion.

117
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:04,300
The draft's list of things that could go wrong ran 94 pages.

118
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:07,500
We're worth more than twice what we were in February.

119
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:10,300
Correct. Congratulations.

120
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:14,600
Then, why does this say we lost $72 billion?

121
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:18,000
Because you're worth more than twice what you were in February.

122
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,400
Remember the I.O.S., Amazon, Google, and other early backers

123
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:25,600
gave Anthropic cash in exchange for shares later.

124
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,300
Picture a lemonade stand.

125
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:30,900
Your neighbor gives you $100 today for a slice of the stand

126
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:32,500
handed over down the road.

127
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:36,100
Then, your stand becomes the hottest stand in America.

128
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:40,000
That slice is now worth a fortune, and you still owe it.

129
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,300
The accounting rules say, the more your promise is worth, the more you owe.

130
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:47,000
And that jump goes on the books as a loss.

131
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,700
Out of the $80.7 billion, $72.4 billion is exactly that.

132
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:54,200
A charge on paper.

133
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:57,000
So, did any actual money leave?

134
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,300
Not for that part.

135
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,000
Look only at the business of selling, Claude,

136
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and it lost about $700 million in those six months.

137
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,300
That's real money.

138
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:09,000
It's also a long way from $80 billion.

139
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,200
And over the first nine months of the year,

140
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:16,500
the business itself brought in $6.6 billion more than it cost to run.

141
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:17,800
Now flip it around.

142
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:20,600
Amazon owns a pile of those IOUs.

143
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:25,500
On its own books, it reported gains of more than $60 billion from them.

144
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:26,500
Same promise.

145
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:31,300
One company calls it a loss, the other calls it a profit, and it keeps going.

146
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:33,700
On November 2nd, a new filing.

147
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:38,100
The loss for the first nine months is $104 billion.

148
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:41,800
That's more than SpaceX raised in its record IPO in June.

149
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:46,300
$94 billion of it is paper, including a $2 billion charge

150
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:48,700
for matching the staff's donations to charity.

151
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:54,100
Meanwhile, Anthropoc is sitting on about $132 billion in cash.

152
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:55,400
More than it lost.

153
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:56,700
Both numbers are true.

154
00:08:56,700 --> 00:09:00,400
So, the scary number on the thumbnail is mostly a scoreboard

155
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,800
of how fast Anthropoc's value went up.

156
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:03,700
Case closed?

157
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:04,800
Not quite.

158
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:08,100
Because buried in the same filing is a different number.

159
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:10,100
And that one is real.

160
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:15,400
$895 billion.

161
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:19,600
That's how much Anthropoc has signed up to pay for computers in the years ahead.

162
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:21,300
It's sales so far this year?

163
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:22,700
$33 billion.

164
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:25,700
The bill is about 27 times bigger.

165
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:27,000
Here's who gets paid.

166
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,800
Broadcom, about $160 billion.

167
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,500
Google, $120 billion.

168
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,400
Amazon, $110 billion.

169
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:35,400
Microsoft, $45 billion.

170
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:39,600
And about $120 billion to SpaceX's AI business.

171
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:41,400
Yes, that's SpaceX.

172
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:46,700
The company whose IPO record Anthropoc is chasing is also one of its landlords.

173
00:09:46,700 --> 00:09:50,200
Some of it can be canceled or depends on conditions.

174
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:52,400
About $214 billion.

175
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:59,300
But $70 billion is due in the next 12 months and almost $450 billion before 2031.

176
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:02,200
Why sign for so much so far ahead?

177
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:05,100
Because compute gets rented years in advance.

178
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:08,000
Book too little and you pay the last-minute price.

179
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,100
That's exactly what happened this summer.

180
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:17,700
From July to September, computers cost Anthropoc $9.9 billion, up 77%.

181
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:22,000
$5 billion of that was expensive capacity grabbed at short notice.

182
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,200
The business slipped back into a small operating loss.

183
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:27,000
We need more computers today.

184
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,400
Today costs more.

185
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:31,100
How much have you got?

186
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:33,700
About $5 billion worth.

187
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:35,500
So that's one way to get it wrong.

188
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:38,300
Buy too little and you pay through the nose.

189
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:44,200
The other way is to buy too much, which brings us to you.

190
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,800
Here's how a company sells itself to the public.

191
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:49,800
First, the filing, then the roadshow.

192
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:54,200
For about a week, bankers pitch the company to the biggest funds on earth.

193
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:55,800
The funds place orders.

194
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:58,000
This many shares at this price.

195
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,200
The night before trading starts, the company and its banks pick the price that fills the book.

196
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:07,700
Then the market opens and the price does whatever it wants.

197
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:09,200
How much did it lose?

198
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:10,700
Which loss?

199
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:11,900
The big one.

200
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:14,200
Oh, that one's good news.

201
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:16,400
Then we'll pay the low end.

202
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:20,900
Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley lead it, with 22 banks in all.

203
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:22,100
One more thing to know.

204
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:25,200
Anthropic only gets the money from new shares.

205
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:29,300
When early holders sell their shares in the deal, that money goes to them.

206
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:32,300
And most insiders can't sell for six months anyway.

207
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:34,700
The founders, for up to about a year.

208
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:37,200
Regular people get a real seat this time.

209
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:41,700
The filing names six brokers, from Schwab and Fidelity to Robinhood.

210
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:45,900
Reports say the public could get 15 to 20% of the shares.

211
00:11:45,900 --> 00:11:51,600
And in September, Anthropic split every share into 10, before the public could buy one.

212
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,400
The price talk is one and a half to two trillion dollars.

213
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:57,500
The big funds are pushing for the low end.

214
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:04,500
Even there, buyers pay about $43 for every dollar of sales Anthropic made over the past 12 months.

215
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:06,700
At two trillion, 57.

216
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:11,600
Jay Ritter, the professor Wall Street calls Mr. IPO, has counted.

217
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:21,200
Out of 4,110 American IPOs from 1980 to 2024, only 14 priced at 40 times sales or more.

218
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:24,800
On average, they did 15% worse than the market.

219
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:29,800
Aswath Damodaran, who values companies for a living, called the latest quarter

220
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:33,300
the most telegraphed third quarter number of all time.

221
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:38,900
He puts Anthropic at about 1.475 trillion, just under the low end.

222
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:42,100
Now, back to February and that podcast.

223
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:48,200
Dario was talking about the danger of a lab buying compute too early, years ahead of the customers.

224
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:49,400
He didn't soften it.

225
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:56,000
If I'm just off by a year in that rate of growth, or if the growth rate is 5x a year instead of 10x a year,

226
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,200
then you go bankrupt.

227
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:59,600
That's not someone betting against the stock.

228
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:04,300
That's the CEO, describing the trap his whole industry is standing in.

229
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:11,200
Anthropic has signed $895 billion of bills on the bet that growth shows up on schedule.

230
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:13,600
Now, it's asking the public to buy in.

231
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:14,900
Here's where it stands.

232
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:21,300
The road show can start as early as November 10th, with trading planned for mid-November, the week of the 16th.

233
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:23,600
The Pentagon fight stands at a split.

234
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:28,700
A judge threw out one label in August and an appeals court kept the other in September.

235
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:33,500
SpaceX, the record holder, is trading right back around its IPO price.

236
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:40,000
And whatever the price, the seven co-founders keep control through one special share, one share,

237
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,500
with 50.1% of the vote.

238
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,500
They've also pledged 80% of their stake to charity.

239
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,700
$80 billion sounds like the end of a company.

240
00:13:48,700 --> 00:13:52,100
Here, it's mostly the receipt for becoming more valuable.

241
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:56,300
The number that decides this story is the one nobody puts on a thumbnail.

242
00:13:56,300 --> 00:14:01,000
$895 billion of computers already booked.

243
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,200
Now the customers have to show up on time.

244
00:14:03,200 --> 00:14:07,100
As Mr. IPO put it, a lot of things have to go right.

